WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.450
Hi, I’m Zan.

2
00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:07.396
Welcome to my multimodal journal,
which I created for my postgraduate

3
00:00:07.446 --> 00:00:09.550
diploma in instructional design.

4
00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:18.304
It follows how my thinking changed as I worked
with theories of multimedia learning,

5
00:00:18.354 --> 00:00:24.350
universal design and instructional design
frameworks over the last year.

6
00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:33.150
I’ve become really interested in
the creative side of this field.

7
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.450
I consider myself a creative person,

8
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:43.950
and instructional design turned out to be
far more creative than it first appeared to me.

9
00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:52.315
Beyond the important theory that underpins
instructional design, creativity and empathy

10
00:00:52.365 --> 00:00:57.350
also shape every decision about
how meaning gets communicated.

11
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.150
And multimodality is where
I see that most clearly.

12
00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:07.467
Each mode has its own strengths,
and learners read them differently,

13
00:01:07.517 --> 00:01:14.950
which makes human-centred design so important,
more than just following a simple formula.

14
00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:20.250
There’s a reason for the collage theme
you’ll see throughout this journal.

15
00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:28.550
I think a collage works the same way
as multimodal instructional design.

16
00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:34.278
It’s made of separate pieces,
arranged and rearranged until

17
00:01:34.328 --> 00:01:36.750
together they tell one story.

18
00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:44.750
It’s layered, developing
and never fully resolved,

19
00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:52.250
and nothing here is meant
to be a final thought either.

20
00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:57.374
It’s simply me working through
my own design identity,

21
00:01:57.424 --> 00:02:02.850
the same way I’d want any learner
to have room to work through theirs.

22
00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:06.850
Thanks for joining me,
and I hope you enjoy exploring.
